\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Self-adjoint fields, Weyl relations and stationary states}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{For the \vocabk{quantum scalar fields}{fdip-00H8} and
\vocabk{conjugate momenta}{fdip-00H8}, each #{\Phi(r)} and #{\Pi(z)}
is self-adjoint on its stated maximal domain, with core
#{C_c^\infty(\mathsf Q)}. For real #{r,z\in\mathsf Q}, the
\vocabk{Weyl operators}{fdip-00H8} are unitary and satisfy
##{\begin{aligned}
W(r,z)^{-1}&=W(-r,-z),\\
W(r,z)&=\ue^{\ui \Phi(r)/2}\ue^{\ui \Pi(z)}\ue^{\ui \Phi(r)/2},\\
W(r,z)W(r',z')
 &=\ue^{-\ui \sigma((r,z),(r',z'))/2}W(r+r',z+z').
\end{aligned}}
The exponentials use the spectral calculus of these self-adjoint
operators.}

\p{The \vocabk{real-time evolution}{fdip-00H8} is a strongly
continuous unitary group. The \vocabk{vacuum state}{fdip-00H8}
and \vocabk{thermal state}{fdip-00H8} are positive, normalized
and stationary under this evolution. For either state, arbitrary
real times and bounded insertions,
##{|\omega(A_0\alpha_{t_1}(A_1)\cdots\alpha_{t_n}(A_n))|
 \le\prod_{j=0}^n\|A_j\|.}
In particular all these correlations of Weyl operators exist
and have absolute value at most one. The potential has the full
continuous coercive hypotheses of \ref{fdip-00H2}; no polynomial
specialization is used.}

\proof{
\p{The real multiplication operator #{\Phi(r)} is self-adjoint on
its maximal domain. The unitary Fourier transform on the Euclidean
space #{\mathsf Q}, identified with its dual by the metric, sends
#{\Pi(z)} to multiplication by #{\langle z,\xi\rangle}, proving
the same assertion for momentum. Cutoff followed by mollification
approximates both a function and its position product in #{L^2};
for momentum it approximates #{f,D_zf} in #{L^2}.
Thus #{C_c^\infty} is a core for each operator, also for the zero
direction. Fourier inversion gives
#{(\ue^{\ui \Pi(z)}f)(q)=f(q+z)}; putting the two half-phase multipliers
around this translation gives the stated exponential factorization.
These are the position and Fourier realizations of
\citet{Section 8.1, pp. 207-208}{teschl2014quantum}.}

\p{Translation invariance of #{dq} and modulus one of the phase
give #{\|W(r,z)f\|=\|f\|}. Direct substitution gives the
inverse #{W(-r,-z)}, so the operator is unitary. Applying two
Weyl actions successively gives the phase
#{\langle r,q+z/2\rangle+\langle r',q+z+z'/2\rangle}.
Subtracting the phase
#{\langle r+r',q+(z+z')/2\rangle} leaves
#{-\sigma((r,z),(r',z'))/2}, proving the multiplication rule.}

\p{Since #{H} is self-adjoint, its spectral calculus gives
#{V_t^*=V_{-t}} and #{V_tV_u=V_{t+u}}. Dominated convergence
of the spectral multipliers #{\ue^{-\ui t\lambda}} proves strong
continuity. The vacuum state is a unit vector state; the thermal
state uses the positive trace-one operator #{\ue^{-\beta H}/Z_\beta},
whose finite trace follows from \ref{fdip-00H3}. Thus both are
positive and normalized. Conjugation by #{V_t} preserves operator
norm, and either state is bounded by that norm; for the thermal
state this follows from the trace norm inequality. Multiplying
the operator-norm bounds gives the correlation estimate.}

\p{The ground vector evolves by the phase #{\ue^{-\ui tE_0}}, and
#{\ue^{-\beta H}} commutes with #{V_t}. Substitution into the
vacuum inner product and cyclicity of the bounded/trace-class
thermal product give #{\omega\circ\alpha_t=\omega}.
The group identity then gives stationarity of the multi-time
correlations under a common time translation.}
}
